      "end": 69,
      "role": "LOCATION_CONSTRAINT"
    },
    {
      "id": "atom-2",
      "text": "Igbo people are the richest people per capita in Africa.",
      "sourceText": "are the richest people per capita in Africa",
      "start": 71,
      "end": 114,
      "role": "CORE"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-a10b02ffdad8::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-a10b02ffdad8",
          "text": "Igbo are predominantly Christian. ",
          "start": 209,
          "end": 243,
          "context": "Their traditional homeland straddles the Niger River in the south-east and is one of the most densely populated areas of the African continent.  Igbo are predominantly Christian.  Traditionally, Igbo were subsistence farmers of yams, taro and cassava. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-a10b02ffdad8::sentence-9",
          "documentId": "doc-a10b02ffdad8",
          "text": "Igbo are believed to have originated as a people several thousand years ago in the area where the Benue flows into the Niger River. ",
          "start": 497,
          "end": 629,
          "context": "Historical context\n Igbo are believed to have originated as a people several thousand years ago in the area where the Benue flows into the Niger River.  The Igbo were active slave traders, selling captives from the interior to European traders. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-a10b02ffdad8::sentence-5",
          "documentId": "doc-a10b02ffdad8",
          "text": "Traditionally, Igbo were subsistence farmers of yams, taro and cassava. ",
          "start": 243,
          "end": 315,
          "context": "Igbo are predominantly Christian.  Traditionally, Igbo were subsistence farmers of yams, taro and cassava.  Today many are well educated and work as civil servants and in business. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f::sentence-9",
          "documentId": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f",
          "text": "Some of the slaves who were exported as a result of the civil wars were sent to Brazil and some other countries outside Nigeria and Africa, hence Yoruba-speaking people can be found in Togo, Benin Republic, Sierra Leone, Liberia and some other African countries.\n",
          "start": 1276,
          "end": 1539,
          "context": "The New World Encyclopedia states that during the decline of the Oyo Empire, Yorubaland degenerated into series of civil war in which military captives were sold as slaves.  Some of the slaves who were exported as a result of the civil wars were sent to Brazil and some other countries outside Nigeria and Africa, hence Yoruba-speaking people can be found in Togo, Benin Republic, Sierra Leone, Liberia and some other African countries.\n Verification\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f::sentence-6",
          "documentId": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f",
          "text": "The Yoruba in the Diaspora, including those based in Brazil, believe Ile Ife, in South West Nigeria, is their ancestral homeland. ",
          "start": 822,
          "end": 952,
          "context": "He said, “I don’t want them to be a lost tribe; that’s why we have been doing a lot of programmes with them. That’s why we have the Ifa Heritage Mobile Museum set up in Rio de Janeiro in Valongo Wharf. That’s the biggest port through which human beings were moved and transitioned.”\n The Yoruba in the Diaspora, including those based in Brazil, believe Ile Ife, in South West Nigeria, is their ancestral homeland.  The Yoruba make up a huge diaspora population largely due to the slave trade, and lately, the emigration from Nigeria linked to socio-economic issues.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f::sentence-21",
          "documentId": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f",
          "text": "Further checks from the New World Encyclopedia, which was last updated on September 10, 2019, put the global population of Yoruba at 19.33 million, primarily from Nigeria, Benin and Togo.\n",
          "start": 3417,
          "end": 3605,
          "context": "However, there is no mention of Yoruba people in the breakdown of the ethnicities in Brazil, and at best, the Yoruba were among the sub-ethnic groups captured under mulatto and black.\n Further checks from the New World Encyclopedia, which was last updated on September 10, 2019, put the global population of Yoruba at 19.33 million, primarily from Nigeria, Benin and Togo.\n Data sourced from the University of Florida’s resource on African Studies indicate that there are 25 million Yoruba globally from Nigeria, Benin, Togo and communities in Brazil, Sierra Leone, northern Ghana and Cuba.\n"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "atomId": "atom-2",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-a10b02ffdad8::sentence-2",
          "documentId": "doc-a10b02ffdad8",
          "text": "Igbo make up around 18 per cent of Nigeria’s population. ",
          "start": 8,
          "end": 65,
          "context": "Profile\n Igbo make up around 18 per cent of Nigeria’s population.  Their traditional homeland straddles the Niger River in the south-east and is one of the most densely populated areas of the African continent. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-a10b02ffdad8::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-a10b02ffdad8",
          "text": "Igbo are predominantly Christian. ",
          "start": 209,
          "end": 243,
          "context": "Their traditional homeland straddles the Niger River in the south-east and is one of the most densely populated areas of the African continent.  Igbo are predominantly Christian.  Traditionally, Igbo were subsistence farmers of yams, taro and cassava. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-a10b02ffdad8::sentence-5",
          "documentId": "doc-a10b02ffdad8",
          "text": "Traditionally, Igbo were subsistence farmers of yams, taro and cassava. ",
          "start": 243,
          "end": 315,
          "context": "Igbo are predominantly Christian.  Traditionally, Igbo were subsistence farmers of yams, taro and cassava.  Today many are well educated and work as civil servants and in business. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f::sentence-21",
          "documentId": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f",
          "text": "Further checks from the New World Encyclopedia, which was last updated on September 10, 2019, put the global population of Yoruba at 19.33 million, primarily from Nigeria, Benin and Togo.\n",
          "start": 3417,
          "end": 3605,
          "context": "However, there is no mention of Yoruba people in the breakdown of the ethnicities in Brazil, and at best, the Yoruba were among the sub-ethnic groups captured under mulatto and black.\n Further checks from the New World Encyclopedia, which was last updated on September 10, 2019, put the global population of Yoruba at 19.33 million, primarily from Nigeria, Benin and Togo.\n Data sourced from the University of Florida’s resource on African Studies indicate that there are 25 million Yoruba globally from Nigeria, Benin, Togo and communities in Brazil, Sierra Leone, northern Ghana and Cuba.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f::sentence-19",
          "documentId": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f",
          "text": "The World Factbook, which details the demographics of the global population, estimates that the Yoruba constitute about 105 million people in total, majority of them from Nigeria, where the Yoruba make up 21 per cent of the country’s population.\n",
          "start": 2987,
          "end": 3233,
          "context": "CIA’s data show that as at 2018, the total population of Brazil was 208.9 million, out of which, by ethnicity, whites made up 47.7 per cent, mulatto (mixed white and black) constituted 43.1 per cent, blacks accounted for 7.6 per cent, Asians for 1.1 per cent while indigenous people for 0.4 per cent.\n The World Factbook, which details the demographics of the global population, estimates that the Yoruba constitute about 105 million people in total, majority of them from Nigeria, where the Yoruba make up 21 per cent of the country’s population.\n However, there is no mention of Yoruba people in the breakdown of the ethnicities in Brazil, and at best, the Yoruba were among the sub-ethnic groups captured under mulatto and black.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f::sentence-16",
          "documentId": "doc-308dd4e1ff1f",
          "text": "The World Factbook puts the population of Yoruba people in Nigeria at 28.28 million, being 13.9 per cent of Nigeria’s population of 203.5 million as at 2018.\n",
          "start": 2412,
          "end": 2570,
          "context": "The World Factbook published by the CIA provides regularly updated information on the history, people, population demographics, society, government, economy, energy, geography, communication, transportation, military and transnational issues for 267 world entities.  The World Factbook puts the population of Yoruba people in Nigeria at 28.28 million, being 13.9 per cent of Nigeria’s population of 203.5 million as at 2018.\n This translates to about three times the number of Yoruba people living in Brazil going by the claim being checked. "
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
